  "text": "Sure! You need a function that processes a string and returns a histogram (a dictionary) of the word frequencies. The function should:\n\n- Convert all text to **lowercase** to ensure **case-insensitive** counting.\n- Ignore **punctuation**.\n- Count only words (split by whitespace).\n\n### Here's a clean and efficient implementation in Python:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation\n    text = text.translate(str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation))\n    \n    # Split into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Count frequencies using a dictionary\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n### Example Usage:\n\n```python\nprint(word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\"))\n# Output: {'hello': 2, 'world': 1}\n\nprint(word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\"))\n# Output: {'python': 2, 'is': 2, 'great': 1, 'fun': 1}\n\nprint(word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\"))\n# Output: {'test': 3}\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- `str.translate` with `str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)` efficiently removes punctuation.\n- The `get` method is used for clarity and efficiency in the dictionary update.\n\nLet me know if you'd like this in another language or with additional features (like handling hyphenated words, etc.).",
